---
mdv: "1.0"
title: MDVX diagnostics
---

# Three ways an expression can be wrong

An expression can fail to parse, it can parse and then be handed the wrong
kinds of value, or it can name a function that does not exist. MDVX reports
each of those differently on purpose, because the fix is different in each
case and the author is reading the report, not the parser.

`revenue * region` parses perfectly well and is only wrong once the data
arrives, so it is a warning that says how many values went null. `revenu(cost)`
is a misspelling the whitelist can catch without any data at all, and the
unclosed `(revenue - cost` never becomes a tree, so the offset of the failure
is the most useful thing to hand back.

```mdv bar
x: region
y: revenue
transform:
  - derive:
      typed: revenue * region
      unknown: revenu(cost)
      malformed: (revenue - cost
---
region,revenue,cost
north,40,25
south,24,12
```

The block fails and says so, and the document around it does not.

```mdv bar
title: The rest of the document is unharmed
x: region
y: revenue
---
region,revenue
north,40
south,24
```
